      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Average Cost by Material"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "While asphalt shingles balance cost and durability well under Utah's heavy UV exposure, other materials offer longer lifespans at a higher price point."
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Asphalt Architectural Shingles ($8,500 \u2013 $18,000): The most common option in Utah due to its affordability and 20-to-30-year lifespan."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Premium / Designer Shingles ($12,000 \u2013 $25,000): Heavier materials offering superior wind resistance and impact protection against hailstorms."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Metal Roofing ($15,000 \u2013 $35,000): Highly durable against heavy winter snow loads and lasts 40 to 70 years."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Flat Roof TPO ($6,000 \u2013 $22,500): Common on modern builds and commercial structures, fluctuating heavily by square footage."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Regional Variations"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Labor markets influence pricing slightly. According to data from regional guides like Homer Roofing , projects in Cache County or Davis County lean closer to the lower end of the average spectrum. Salt Lake County projects tend to run about $500 to $2,000 higher due to tighter labor markets and code complexity. Local calculators from platforms like peg the typical Salt Lake City metro average right around $14,500.",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
            "text": "Homer Roofing",
            "link": "https://homerroofing.net/learning-center/roof-replacement-cost-utah/"
          },
          {
            "link": "https://realcostiq.com/cost-guides/roof-replacement/salt-lake-city-ut/"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Hidden Cost Drivers"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Pitch & Stories: Steep rooflines or multi-story homes require extra safety equipment and labor, raising costs by 15% to 30%."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Tear-off Layers: Removing more than one layer of old shingles adds $1,000 to $3,000 in disposal fees."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Decking Repairs: Replacing rotted OSB/plywood sheets found after tear-off adds roughly $60 to $80 per sheet."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "To help narrow down your estimate, could you share the approximate square footage of your home, the current roofing material, and whether your roof has a steep slope or multiple levels?"
      }
